  <a href="#section-configure" id="section-configure" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Configure the relationship</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
    <p>Build one Mailer for each approved relay. Give each its own credentials and settings,
      and attach the backup to the primary. The invoice worker sends through <code>primary</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">FailoverPolicy policy = FailoverPolicy.builder()
    .withPrimaryRecheckInterval(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
    .build();

try (Mailer alternative = mail.mailerBuilder()
         .withSMTPServer("secondary.example.org", 587)
         .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
         .buildMailer();
     Mailer primary = mail.mailerBuilder()
         .withSMTPServer("primary.example.org", 587)
         .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
         .withFailoverMailer(alternative, policy)
         .buildMailer()) {
    primary.async().sendMail(email).getCompletion().join();
}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="wide">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Use either send mode.</strong> The same relationship works with <code>primary.sync().sendMail(email)</code>, with or without the batch module.
          Pooling reuses connections; it is not required to enable failover. Add each server's credentials and other settings to its own builder.</li>
        <li><strong>Replace or remove a fallback.</strong> <code>withFailoverMailer(mailer, policy)</code> replaces the destination and policy together.
          <code>clearFailoverMailer()</code> removes both, unless centrally supplied configuration locks prevent that change.</li>
        <li><strong>Add another relay if needed.</strong> You can give B its own relationship to C. One operation follows that route with one deadline, cancellation control, completion and observer.
          It never recursively calls B's public send method or moves the job into B's executor.</li>
        <li><strong>Close the Mailers you create.</strong> Supplied Mailers are borrowed: closing A does not close B. Close them yourself after their work has drained, as the example does.
          Mailers privately built from named properties are owned and closed by their parent. A participant cannot close itself from a send callback
          that is still using it. A foreign implementation of the <code>Mailer</code> interface cannot be composed this way; an SJM-created Mailer
          using another Jakarta Mail provider can, where the provider exposes enough evidence to identify connection failures.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-ownership" id="section-ownership" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Which Mailer owns what?</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Suppose you call A and the selected connection belongs to B. B's settings matter for more than its hostname.</p>
    <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable failover ownership matrix"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Owner</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Executor, admission, observer, result, cancellation and overall deadline</td><td>A, the initiating Mailer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ordinary Email defaults/overrides, validation and message protection</td><td>B, the actual selected Mailer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Credentials, TLS, proxy, Session, provider, size and content settings</td><td>B</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sending allowance</td><td>The selected SMTP configuration; switching destinations doesn't reset its history</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shared cluster pool settings</td><td>The existing first-registration owner</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mandatory message locks</td><td>A, traversed failover Mailers, and the selected cluster member together</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Connection-setting locks</td><td>The actual approved destination</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Apply the selected relay’s template.</strong> Each candidate starts from your original Email. If A normally adds <code>X-Relay: primary</code> and B adds
        <code>X-Relay: secondary</code>, B gets only its own ordinary template. A's ordinary BCC recipients do not travel with it either.
        This selected-member rule also applies to cluster load balancing without failover.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose who gets an operations copy.</strong> B can have a maintainer BCC of its own, so staff get a copy only when B sends. That is an actual recipient: consider confidentiality,
        recipient limits and cost. A mandatory archive BCC supplied through <code>simplejavamail.locked.defaults.bcc.address</code> still applies,
        in addition to B's ordinary BCC. Conflicting mandatory message settings stop the operation.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep locks and exact bytes.</strong> Defaults, overrides and per-property suppression keep their normal precedence within the selected Mailer. Exact emails still bypass ordinary
        templates, and their authoritative bytes are not rewritten. If mandatory message settings cannot be honored without altering protected or exact
        content, the operation fails. See <a href="/configuration.html">configuration</a> and <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-exact-eml">exact EML submission</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Check what accepted the mail.</strong> A <code>CustomMailer</code> remains an opaque callback and is never invoked again automatically. Logging-only sends don't try SMTP connections.
        Do not configure either as a delivery fallback and assume it proves SMTP acceptance.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-policy" id="section-policy" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Choose failure modes and recovery timing</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>When your invoice worker cannot connect to relay A, first decide which failures should let it try the approved backup B.</p>
    <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="How failover handles connection failures"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>What failed on A</th><th>What the Mailer does</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>DNS lookup, connection refusal, unreachable endpoint, timeout or disconnect during setup, or a temporary service-unavailable setup reply</td>
          <td>Can try B with the default policy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rejected credentials, such as SMTP <code>535</code></td>
          <td>Stops by default. Set <code>withFailoverOnAuthenticationFailure(true)</code> to allow B.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Certificate or hostname verification</td>
          <td>Stops by default. Set <code>withFailoverOnCertificateVerificationFailure(true)</code> if your deployment approves B.
            A still fails verification. B must pass its own trust, hostname, and mandatory TLS checks.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Invalid configuration, failed validation, conflicting locks, missing required capabilities, unknown setup failure, full pool
          until claim timeout, cancellation, shutdown, or the overall send deadline</td>
          <td>Stops this send.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>

    <h3>Follow an outage until the primary recovers</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>First send.</strong> A refuses the connection. With the default one attempt, the Mailer can try B immediately.
        Several configured setup attempts would let it try A again before moving on.</li>
      <li><strong>Before the recheck.</strong> New sends can use B under B's own sending limits. The 30-second default recheck
        interval starts after A's last failed attempt.</li>
      <li><strong>When the recheck is due.</strong> One send tries A while others can keep using B. If A connects, it becomes preferred again.
        If A still fails, the next recheck is due after another interval, or a longer one when backoff is enabled.</li>
    </ol>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Configure setup attempts and recheck timing</summary>
      <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable failover timing reference"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Builder method</th><th>Default</th><th>What it changes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
          <tr><td><code>withPrimaryConnectionAttempts(n)</code></td><td>1</td><td>Total setup attempts per candidate during this visit, including the first.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>withPrimaryConnectionRetryDelay(duration)</code></td><td>Zero</td><td>Wait between additional attempts you explicitly enable.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>withPrimaryRecheckInterval(duration)</code></td><td>30 seconds</td><td>When a failed destination becomes eligible for another check.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>withPrimaryRecheckBackoff(multiplier, maximum)</code></td><td>Disabled</td><td>Increase the interval after each failed recheck, up to the supplied maximum.</td></tr>
        </tbody></table></div>
      <p>A failed recheck increases the interval once, even if it makes several connection attempts. A recheck happens when a send needs
        a destination. There is no background health probe, and pool replenishment stops trying the failed destination during the interval.</p>
      <p>Every policy setting has a matching reset method. <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().getFailoverPolicy()</code> returns the resolved
        policy when a relationship is configured. Before building, <code>mailerBuilder.getFailoverPolicy()</code> returns the stored policy and
        <code>getFailoverMailer()</code> returns a directly supplied Mailer. The Mailer getter returns <code>null</code> for a named
        destination. Inspecting the builder leaves that destination unbuilt.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>AUTH errors, clustered routes, and recovery history</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>AUTH outages.</strong> Managed Angus can distinguish a setup timeout, disconnect, or SMTP <code>421</code> during AUTH
          from rejected credentials. Those outages follow the ordinary setup policy. A caller-supplied plain Angus Session keeps its existing
          behavior. If its exception gives no reliable distinction, credential-failure failover still requires explicit permission.</li>
        <li><strong>Clusters.</strong> The cluster's strategy selects the first candidate. Eligible setup failures can exhaust the captured members
          before the configured failover relationship is used. A healthy but busy pool still waits for capacity. Registering or retiring pools while
          a send is running does not extend that send's candidate traversal indefinitely.</li>
        <li><strong>Recovery history.</strong> A factory tracks failures by configured destination identity. Repeated construction from one named
          definition shares the relevant history and sending allowance. A shared hostname, another factory, or another process does not share that
          history. Each caller still needs its own permission to fail over on credential or certificate failures.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-named" id="section-named" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Configure named destinations and locks</h2>
  <section><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.smtp.host=primary.example.org
simplejavamail.smtp.port=587
simplejavamail.transportstrategy=SMTP_TLS

simplejavamail.failover.mailer=secondary
simplejavamail.failover.primary.connectionattempts=1
simplejavamail.failover.primary.retrydelay=PT0S
simplejavamail.failover.primary.recheckinterval=PT30S
simplejavamail.failover.primary.backoffmultiplier=1
simplejavamail.failover.primary.maximumrecheckinterval=PT30S
simplejavamail.failover.onauthenticationfailure=false
simplejavamail.failover.oncertificateverificationfailure=false

simplejavamail.mailers.secondary.smtp.host=secondary.example.org
simplejavamail.mailers.secondary.smtp.port=587
simplejavamail.mailers.secondary.transportstrategy=SMTP_TLS
simplejavamail.mailers.secondary.defaults.bcc.address=mail-operations@example.org</code></pre>
  </div><div class="wide">
    <p>Named definitions use the existing property tails and resolve independently. Secondary does not inherit primary's ordinary settings or credentials.
      For a further relationship, set <code>simplejavamail.mailers.secondary.failover.mailer=third</code> and define
      <code>simplejavamail.mailers.third.*</code>. Missing references, cycles and known conflicting locks are rejected before private resources are built.</p>
    <p>A multiplier of one means fixed reconsideration intervals. A larger multiplier requires an explicit maximum. Policy properties without
      <code>failover.mailer</code> are incomplete configuration and are rejected.</p>
    <p>The same names work through the <a href="/spring.html">Spring Environment integration</a>, including nested YAML. A central configuration owner
      can lock the route and a destination's TLS settings:</p>
  </div><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.locked.failover.mailer=secondary
simplejavamail.locked.mailers.secondary.transportstrategy=SMTP_TLS
simplejavamail.locked.defaults.bcc.address=archive@example.org</code></pre>
  </div><div class="wide">
    <p>A locked relationship protects the captured permitted definition and its nested relationships, not just the text “secondary”. Java code cannot
      substitute a different Mailer with that label. Policy fields can be locked separately. A's locked SMTP host stays A's host; choosing approved B
      does not edit A's connection configuration. Locks prevent accidental changes within this factory, not deliberate use of an unrelated factory.</p>
    <p>The CLI uses generated named-selection options. Put the named definitions in your loaded properties and select a route with
      <code>--mailer:withFailoverMailerName secondary</code>, optionally adding
      <code>--mailer:withFailoverPrimaryRecheckInterval PT30S</code>. The command still waits for its send to finish.
      See <a href="/cli.html">CLI configuration</a>; Java callers use the paired Mailer/policy API, not these internal text-based methods.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-batches" id="section-batches" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Recover during a large batch</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>A billing job sends three invoices through <code>primary.sync().sendMailsInSimpleBatch(invoices)</code>.
      A goes down just before the job starts, then comes back while the batch is still running.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>First invoice.</strong> A cannot connect, so B connects and sends it. The batch keeps B's connection.</li>
      <li><strong>Second invoice.</strong> A's recheck interval hasn't elapsed. B sends this invoice under its own sending limits,
        even if A has already come back online.</li>
      <li><strong>Third invoice.</strong> A's recheck is now due, so the batch can try A before submitting this invoice. If A connects,
        it sends under A's limits. If the check fails, the batch can keep using its eligible connection to B.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The switch happens between emails, after the previous email and its observer handoff finish. The Mailer opens the iterator once.
      A submission failure still stops the batch. A message already handed to an SMTP server stays on that server.</p>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Retaining connections and cleaning up</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pool lease or direct connection.</strong> With failover and <code>batch-module</code>, a simple batch or
          <code>withOpenConnection(...)</code> retains a lease from the selected pool. The scope occupies that capacity even while
          application code runs or per-email sending limits make it wait. When it ends, a healthy lease is released for reuse and an
          unhealthy one is invalidated. Without <code>batch-module</code> or without failover, the scope opens and closes a dedicated connection.
          Per-email diagnostic timings avoid counting shared setup and final teardown again for each email.</li>
        <li><strong>Pool capacity and idle time.</strong> A size-one pool cannot lend a second connection while the scope has the first.
          If a callback or inline observer needs to send more mail through the same pool, use the callback's sender where available or
          schedule independent work after the scope ends. Allow for rate-limit waits when setting the server's idle timeout. Failover
          cannot reconnect a pinned callback or bypass sending limits. See the <a href="/email-workload-field-guide.html">email workload field guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Open-connection callbacks.</strong> Initial acquisition can fail over. The callback runs once on its chosen connection.
          Use its sender only on the callback's thread before it returns. Use <code>mailer.async().sendMail(...)</code> for independent async sends.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>

    <details class="doc-disclosure">
      <summary>Cancellation and lazy sources</summary>
      <p>A known cancellation or expired deadline stops the batch before another lazy-source callback. The Mailer checks again after an
        observer or connection selection. Successful sends keep their results. Cancellation from the final email's observer still ends
        the batch before it checks for another email. See <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-send-deadlines">batch cancellation</a>
        for what happens when a callback is already running.</p>
    </details>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-observation" id="section-observation" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>See what happened</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>New connection incidents are logged at ERROR, meaningful failover/recovery at INFO, and repeated failed rechecks at DEBUG.
      Factory-local suppression keeps each send from reporting the same outage as a new incident. A successful failover still produces a successful
      send outcome, not a separate application failure callback.</p>
    <p>With a <code>MailSendResultObserver</code>, an individual send also records its connection route:</p>
  </div><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">.withSendHandlers(MailSendHandlers.builder().withResultObserver(outcome -> outcome.getDiagnostics().ifPresent(report -> {
    report.getConnectionHistory().forEach(step -> {
        System.out.printf("attempted=%s acquired=%s elapsed=%s detail=%s%n",
            step.isConnectionAttempted(), step.isConnectionAcquired(),
            step.getElapsed(), step.getExplanation());
    });
    System.out.println("omitted steps=" + report.getOmittedConnectionSteps());
})).build())</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text small">attempted=true  acquired=false elapsed=Optional[PT0.02S] detail=Optional[Connection unavailable]
attempted=true  acquired=true  elapsed=Optional[PT0.08S] detail=Optional.empty
omitted steps=0</code></pre>
  </div><div class="wide">
    <p>A later send might instead show <code>attempted=false</code> for A: it was skipped because of an earlier failure, not contacted again.
      History retains the first 63 steps and the latest step; the omitted count describes anything in between. This reporting bound never limits routing.
      Optional configured-name and logical host/port getters let your application add endpoint labels deliberately. Failed and skipped entries retain
      explicit provider-visible port choices, including a typed direct port above an inherited String default. Unspecified ports remain unavailable;
      these facts do not identify a physical peer. Automatic rendering excludes them.</p>
    <p>Async scheduling includes admission and executor waiting. Preparation starts during execution, after selection, and accumulates governance and
      validation across candidates. Total elapsed time includes orchestration and retry delays, so the measurements need not sum to the total.
      <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-send-timings">Send diagnostics</a> explain the other boundaries.</p>
    <p>For a separate diagnostic check, probe A or B explicitly with <code>mailer.sync().probeConnection(true)</code>. Probing answers whether that
      configured server can connect now; it does not follow the send failover chain or spend sending allowance.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#section-retry" id="section-retry" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Schedule message retries in your application</h2>
  <section><div class="wide">
    <p>Connection failover and a later message retry solve different problems. Use your job system to decide when to try again, keep a bounded attempt
      count and backoff, and correlate the outcome to the original job. An observer receives an outcome, not the original Email or a durable job.</p>
    <p>This sketch hands work to an application queue. <code>jobs</code> and <code>retryQueue</code> are application services, not library APIs:</p>
  </div><div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">.withSendHandlers(MailSendHandlers.builder().withResultObserver(outcome -> {
    if (outcome.isSuccessful()) {
        return;
    }
    outcome.getSubmissionReceipt().ifPresent(receipt -> {
        switch (receipt.getRetryDisposition()) {
            case SAFE_TO_RETRY_ALL:
            case SAFE_TO_RETRY_UNACCEPTED:
                retryQueue.scheduleAfterBackoff(
                    jobs.findByMessageId(outcome.getEffectiveMessageId()),
                    receipt.getRetryableRecipients());
                break;
            default:
                // Unknown acceptance, permanent rejection or another policy decision:
                // don't automatically submit the original message again.
                break;
        }
    });
}).build())</code></pre>
  </div><div class="wide">
    <p>Build any later submission for only the recipients the job policy permits. Never blindly replay a partially accepted message, and stop automatic
      retries when acceptance is uncertain. Also check locks and template-added recipients: a mandatory archive BCC can receive another copy even when
      the application's recipient subset excludes already accepted addresses. Read the <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-retry-decisions">receipt guidance</a>.</p>
    <p>No receipt is not itself permission to retry. Local validation/configuration errors need fixing; exhausted connection routes may warrant delayed
      rescheduling after the underlying cause has been assessed. An accepted receipt followed by cleanup failure must not become a duplicate send.</p>
    <p>Keep inline observer work short: enqueue the decision and return. Alternatively use the existing observer executor option. Do not sleep or resend
      synchronously inside the callback. This recipe runs after terminal completion, including exhaustion of any configured
      <a href="/mail-send-retries.html">built-in retry policy</a>. A durable retry queue remains application-owned.</p>
  </div></section>
